From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Subject: [PATCH] window: allocate the styled shadow for inset maximized windows

Luma maximizes to an inset work area, and LumaUI keeps its ordinary rounded
frame and elevation there. get_shadow_width discarded the CSS shadow extents
for every maximized window, so the buffer ended at the frame and only shadow
fragments inside the rounded corners could survive.

Calculate the actual style extents for a maximized CSD window too. Do not add
resize-handle margins to it: maximizing still disables resizing. A maximized
style with no outer shadow continues to have zero extents, and fullscreen
continues to have none regardless of style. The compositor's frame rectangle,
maximize state, work-area inset and restore geometry remain unchanged.

--- a/gtk/gtkwindow.c
+++ b/gtk/gtkwindow.c
@@ -4236,8 +4236,7 @@ get_shadow_width (GtkWindow *window,
       !priv->use_client_shadow)
     goto out;
 
-  if (priv->maximized ||
-      priv->fullscreen)
+  if (priv->fullscreen)
     goto out;
 
   style = gtk_css_node_get_style (gtk_widget_get_css_node (GTK_WIDGET (window)));
@@ -4245,6 +4244,12 @@ get_shadow_width (GtkWindow *window,
   /* Calculate the size of the drop shadows ... */
   gtk_css_shadow_value_get_extents (style->used->box_shadow, shadow_width);
 
+  /* A maximized window may still have a styled shadow in an inset work area,
+   * but it has no resize handles. In particular, shadow:none stays zero.
+   */
+  if (priv->maximized)
+    return;
+
   shadow_width->left = MAX (shadow_width->left, RESIZE_HANDLE_SIZE);
   shadow_width->top = MAX (shadow_width->top, RESIZE_HANDLE_SIZE);
   shadow_width->bottom = MAX (shadow_width->bottom, RESIZE_HANDLE_SIZE);
